Speed and velocity are two fundamental concepts in physics, particularly in the realm of motion. The key difference between them lies in their magnitude and direction.

Velocity refers to the rate of change of an object's position with respect to time. It is a vector quantity, meaning it has both magnitude (amount of change) and direction. Velocity is often represented by the letter "v" and takes the form of the integral of time-displacement function v(t) from t = 0 to t = v(t).

Speed, on the other hand, is the rate at which an object is moving or is changing its position with respect to time. It is a non-negative scalar quantity, meaning it has no direction. Speed can be thought of as the speed at which an object is moving, or the rate at which it is moving.

Let's consider an example to illustrate the difference. Imagine two objects, A and B, moving at a constant velocity of 5 meters per second in the same direction. If we define speed as the rate at which they are moving, say 10 meters per second, then object A will move at a speed of 5 + 10 = 15 meters per second relative to object B.

Now, if we define speed as the rate at which object A is moving, say 15 meters per second, then object A will move at a speed of 5 + 15 = 20 meters per second relative to object B.

In this example, the velocity is the speed of object B relative to object A, while the speed is the rate of change of object A's position with respect to time, regardless of the direction. In this case, object B is moving at a velocity of 15 meters per second, and object A is moving at a speed of 20 meters per second.

In general, speed is a non-negative scalar quantity, whereas velocity is a scalar quantity that takes into account both direction and magnitude. This difference makes speed a more fundamental concept in physics, as it is the key to understanding the motion of objects in motion and the velocity of objects in motion.
